8.79, "poll_weight": 0.25, "fundamentals": {"margin": 10.5, "lean": 0.0, "environment": 7.9, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.6}, "rating_margin": 8.17, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.84, "baseline_margin": 7.56, "analyst_adjustment": -0.56, "key_factors": ["Incumbent Vasquez with large cash advantage ($2.4M vs $0.8M)", "Expert consensus of Likely/Lean D across nine raters", "Prediction markets at 90-94% for Democrats", "Thin polling: only one recent poll showing a 3-point race"], "rationale": "The baseline model's Likely D call (7.6-point margin, 0.89 win probability) aligns with expert ratings and markets, and Vasquez's fundraising edge and incumbent perks support it. The lone September poll showing just a 3-point race argues for trimming slightly, but not enough to move far from the fundamentals.", "watch": "The October 16 debate and any late Republican outside spending following Mike Johnson's nationalized push could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Gabe Vasquez (D) is running."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's 2nd District is a true swing seat with an even partisan lean, but first-term incumbent Gabe Vasquez holds most of the structural advantages. He has out-raised Republican Greg Cunningham by more than two to one and holds a large cash-on-hand edge, with no outside spending against him so far. Expert raters lean Democratic, ranging from Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets put his win odds around 90 percent.\n\nThe race is not without risk: the only recent poll shows a narrow 3-point Vasquez lead, and national Republicans have invested attention, with Speaker Mike Johnson campaigning for Cunningham. Still, the fundamentals, money, incumbency, and a favorable rating consensus point to a Vasquez win, with enough uncertainty to keep this competitive rather than safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8892, "margin": 7.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8699, "margin": 7.0}]}